<BlogPost Post="BlogBook.MakingLanguagesInRust.Posts[2]">
    @Introduction

    <Paragraph>
        Before we generate code we'll briefly go over your Compiler 101 material to make sure we're on the same page
        when I use terms like &ldquo;SSA&rdquo; or &ldquo;IR&rdquo;.
        We'll then have what it takes to create the Toy dialect and generate some code,
        although fully integrating with the MLIR framework will need to wait until next chapter.
    </Paragraph>

    <Header2>So You Want to Write a Compiler</Header2>

    <Paragraph>
        Compilers take some program in a high-level representation and convert it to a low-level representation,
        such as machine code.<Footnote>We could be debating the exact definition of &ldquo;compiler&rdquo; the whole day,
            this one works for us here. Don't at me with whether or not a transpiler is a compiler.
        </Footnote>
        For example, you might write a <Code>for</Code> loop in a source language:
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" Code=@(@"
fn loopx(n: u64) {
  for i in 0..n {
    doStuff(i);
  }
}")>
    </CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        In the end, you want to compile to, for example, x86 machine code, which might get you assembly like:
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="nasm" LanguageString="x86 asm" Code=@(@"
rust_playground::loopx:
	testq	%rdi, %rdi
	je	.LBB1_4
	pushq	%r15
	pushq	%r14
	pushq	%rbx
	movq	%rdi, %rbx
	xorl	%edi, %edi
	movq	rust_playground::doStuff@GOTPCREL(%rip), %r14

.LBB1_2:
	leaq	1(%rdi), %r15
	callq	*%r14
	movq	%r15, %rdi
	cmpq	%r15, %rbx
	jne	.LBB1_2
	popq	%rbx
	popq	%r14
	popq	%r15

.LBB1_4:
	retq
")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph GutterBottom="false">
        Much lower level of abstraction, not something you'd want to write by hand. To achieve this job, the compiler
        usually goes through at least one
        <Highlight>intermediate representation (IR)</Highlight>
        . In some sense,
        the AST is already an intermediate representation &ndash; pretty close to source code, but less human-friendly
        and more machine-friendly. However, when we say &ldquo;IR&rdquo; in a compiler context we usually mean something
        like LLVM IR.
    </Paragraph>
    <Header3>LLVM IR</Header3>
    <Paragraph>
        LLVM IR is a generic IR that can be targeted by many compilers, for example clang compiles C++ through LLVM IR
        while rustc compiles Rust through LLVM IR.
        For the above, rustc generates something like:
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="llvm" Code=@(@"
; rust_playground::loopx
; Function Attrs: noinline nonlazybind uwtable
define void @_ZN10rust_playground5loopx17h4f38567e5b8723edE(i64 noundef %n) unnamed_addr #0 {
start:
  %_52.not = icmp eq i64 %n, 0
  br i1 %_52.not, label %bb4, label %bb3

bb4:                                              ; preds = %bb3, %start
  ret void

bb3:                                              ; preds = %start, %bb3
  %iter.sroa.0.03 = phi i64 [ %_7, %bb3 ], [ 0, %start ]
  %_7 = add nuw i64 %iter.sroa.0.03, 1
; call rust_playground::doStuff
  tail call void @_ZN10rust_playground7doStuff17h654e5ff735743970E(i64 noundef %iter.sroa.0.03)
  %exitcond.not = icmp eq i64 %_7, %n
  br i1 %exitcond.not, label %bb4, label %bb3
}
")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        This is on a higher level of abstraction than the assembly code before, even if it looks worse due to verbosity.
        For example, it has a conditional branch with two targets at the end instead of the usual assembly
        where we have only one jump target. There are no arbitrary labels &ndash; instead, all code is organised into
        <Highlight>basic blocks</Highlight>
        . A basic block has an entry, executes some code, and then has a single
        <Highlight>terminator</Highlight>
        which determines where the control goes. In the example above, the basic
        blocks defined are <Code>start</Code>, <Code>bb3</Code>, and <Code>bb4</Code>; the
        <Code>br</Code> operation is a terminator that follows to some other basic block, while the <Code>ret</Code>
        terminator exits the entire function. This constrained structure is very useful, as it allows us to talk
        about a function as a
        <Highlight>Control Flow Graph (CFG)</Highlight>
        , where basic blocks are vertices
        and the terminators define the edges. One can do all kinds of fun static analysis on such a graph, tracking
        uses and definitions of a given value, etc.
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph>
        An important property of LLVM IR is that it's in
        <Highlight>Static Single Assignment (SSA) form</Highlight>.
        It means that each value (the names prefixed with <Code>%</Code>) is defined in exactly one lexical place.
        Contrast it with the assembly where the value residing in the <Code>r15</Code> register gets modified in each
        iteration. To achieve mutation, we can use explicit memory operations (stores and loads), or the
        <Code>phi</Code> function which resolves to a different value based on the basic block we entered from.
        This function is magical, in the sense that there's no corresponding machine-level instruction that would
        implement it &ndash; the compiler has to eliminate those phis at some stage during code generation.
    </Paragraph>
    <Header3>Intermediate Intermediate Representations</Header3>
    <Paragraph>
        Now I don't know about you, but for me there is still a gigantic gap between the Rust source we saw first
        and the LLVM IR. The LLVM IR is quite close to the x86 assembly &ndash; I can believe you could turn
        one into the other given lots of time and coffee &ndash; but to turn an AST directly into the IR seems harder.
        Rust actually goes through another IR before the LLVM IR, called the Mid-level IR (MIR).
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" LanguageString="MIR" Code=@(@"
// WARNING: This output format is intended for human consumers only
// and is subject to change without notice. Knock yourself out.
// HINT: See also -Z dump-mir for MIR at specific points during compilation.
fn loopx(_1: u64) -> () {
    debug n => _1;
    let mut _0: ();
    let mut _2: std::option::Option<u64>;
    let _4: ();
    let mut _9: u64;
    scope 1 {
        debug ((iter: std::ops::Range<u64>).0: u64) => _9;
        debug ((iter: std::ops::Range<u64>).1: u64) => _1;
        let _3: u64;
        scope 2 {
            debug i => _3;
        }
        scope 4 (inlined iter::range::<impl Iterator for std::ops::Range<u64>>::next) {
            scope 5 (inlined <std::ops::Range<u64> as iter::range::RangeIteratorImpl>::spec_next) {
                let mut _5: bool;
                let _6: u64;
                let mut _7: u64;
                scope 6 {
                    scope 8 (inlined <u64 as Step>::forward_unchecked) {
                        scope 9 (inlined #[track_caller] core::num::<impl u64>::unchecked_add) {
                            scope 10 (inlined core::ub_checks::check_language_ub) {
                                scope 11 (inlined core::ub_checks::check_language_ub::runtime) {
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
                scope 7 (inlined std::cmp::impls::<impl PartialOrd for u64>::lt) {
                    let mut _8: u64;
                }
            }
        }
    }
    scope 3 (inlined <std::ops::Range<u64> as IntoIterator>::into_iter) {
    }

    bb0: {
        StorageLive(_9);
        _9 = const 0_u64;
        goto -> bb1;
    }

    bb1: {
        StorageLive(_2);
        StorageLive(_5);
        StorageLive(_8);
        _8 = copy _9;
        _5 = Lt(move _8, copy _1);
        StorageDead(_8);
        switchInt(move _5) -> [0: bb4, otherwise: bb3];
    }

    bb2: {
        StorageDead(_2);
        goto -> bb1;
    }

    bb3: {
        _6 = copy _9;
        StorageLive(_7);
        _7 = AddUnchecked(copy _6, const 1_u64);
        _9 = move _7;
        StorageDead(_7);
        _2 = Option::<u64>::Some(copy _6);
        StorageDead(_5);
        _3 = copy ((_2 as Some).0: u64);
        _4 = doStuff(move _3) -> [return: bb2, unwind continue];
    }

    bb4: {
        StorageDead(_5);
        StorageDead(_2);
        StorageDead(_9);
        return;
    }
}
")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        If you ignore the liveness tracking with <Code>StorageLive</Code>/<Code>StorageDead</Code> it's actually quite
        understandable if you know Rust. It's much closer to the actual Rust source, but makes many things more
        explicit and verbose &ndash; it already separates the code into basic blocks, makes branching explicit, etc.
        There's actually one more IR (High-level IR, HIR) before this:
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" LanguageString="HIR" Code=@(@"
fn loopx(n:
        u64) {
    {
        let _t =
            match #[lang = ""into_iter""](#[lang = ""Range""] { start: 0, end: n
                        }) {
                mut iter =>
                    loop {
                        match #[lang = ""next""](&mut iter) {
                            #[lang = ""None""] {} => break,
                            #[lang = ""Some""] {  0: i } => { unsafe { doStuff(i); } }
                        }
                    },
            };
        _t
    }
}")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        Now it's a bit easier how we can go from Rust to assembly &ndash; we actually go Rust &#8594; AST &#8594; HIR &#8594; MIR
        &#8594; LLVM IR &#8594; x86 assembly. Each step along the way is called a <Highlight>lowering</Highlight>.
    </Paragraph>
    <Header3>Why Do This?</Header3>
    <Paragraph>
        Aside from the clear fact that it's easier to lower gradually than go straight for machine code, it also makes
        optimisations much easier. For example, at the HIR level it's still clear that we have a for loop &ndash;
        an iterator over a <Code>Range</Code> from <Code>0</Code> to <Code>n</Code>. The same loop exists at the LLVM IR
        level, but the compiler would have to do work to recognise it as a loop, identify the induction variable
        (<Code>i</Code>), infer its bounds, etc. In general, some optimisations are easier to perform at the high-level,
        while language-specific constructs are still clearly defined, while some optimisations make the most sense
        near the end of the pipeline. If we weren't compiling Rust but a more domain-specific language,
        high-level analysis of some IR would enable many more optimisation opportunities than looking at LLVM IR
        or assembly.Because of this, basically any optimising compiler for a high-level language
        first goes through some kind of IR. Turns out, those IRs share many characteristics,
        and some analysis passes are needed virtually always &ndash; e.g. dead code pruning, liveness analysis.
        This is the key insight behind MLIR &ndash; can we have a shared platform for building your own IRs without
        reinventing the wheel each time?
    </Paragraph>
    <Header2>MLIR</Header2>
    <Paragraph>
        Custom IRs are what MLIR is all about. The MLIR infrastructure is designed to accommodate essentially
        arbitrary IR extensions, as long as they are in SSA form.
        A custom IR is called a
        <Highlight>dialect</Highlight>
        and the core unit in their construction
        is an <Highlight>operation</Highlight>.
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph>
        There are essentially two ways to define custom dialects and operations: manually inheriting from C++ classes
        <Code Language="cpp">mlir::Dialect</Code> and <Code Language="cpp">mlir::Op</Code>, or using the declarative
        Operation Definition Specification
        framework (ODS)<Footnote IsRef="true"><LinkA Href="https://mlir.llvm.org/docs/DefiningDialects/Operations/">MLIR docs: Operation Definition Specification (ODS)</LinkA></Footnote>
        where we provide a TableGen<Footnote IsRef="true"><LinkA Href="https://llvm.org/docs/TableGen/">LLVM docs: TableGen</LinkA></Footnote>
        definition and then use a code generator
        to create the appropriate structures.<Footnote>
            TableGen is an in-house LLVM solution for code generation.
            It turns out that for any sufficiently complex project the chance people invent a declarative DSL
            to make their lives easier tends towards $1$ with time.
        </Footnote> It should go without saying which one of these is considered more pleasing
        in the eyes of the gods.
    </Paragraph>

    <Paragraph GutterBottom="false">
        Melior contains a <Code Language="rust">dialect</Code>
        macro which generates Rust code from <Code>.td</Code> definition
        files.<Footnote IsRef="true"><LinkA Href="https://mlir-rs.github.io/melior/melior/macro.dialect.html">Melior docs: <Code Language="Rust">melior::dialect</Code> macro</LinkA></Footnote>
        This is an excellent idea, although it will quickly turn out the existing generator is lackluster.
        In the end, we will be generating both Rust
        and C++ code from TableGen and then gluing them together &ndash; the C++ side is needed to register our dialect
        and integrate it into the larger MLIR framework, while the Rust side will be used to implement actual logic for
        Toy. In this chapter, however, we will just create our custom dialect and generate some code in it without
        worrying about the rest of MLIR framework or touching any C++. Enjoy it while it lasts!
    </Paragraph>

    <Header2>The Toy Dialect</Header2>

    <Paragraph>
        Let's briefly go through the operations we expect from our dialect. The point of MLIR and custom dialects
        is that we can create an IR at an abstraction level very close to the source language; this enables
        optimisations that would be hard or impossible on either the raw AST or a lower level IR (e.g. the LLVM IR),
        and then allows us to progressively lower from this dialect until we reach the output representation.
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph>
        As an example, a <Code Language="llvm">toy.transpose</Code> operation which transposes a tensor (reverses
        the list of dimensions) might be represented in MLIR assembly as:
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="llvm" LanguageString="MLIR" Code=@(@"
%0 = ""toy.transpose""(%arg0) : (tensor<*xf64>) -> tensor<*xf64> loc(""toy/valid/ch2.toy"":6:10)
")>
    </CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        The left-hand-side is a value. In SSA each value has a unique name in a given code region and cannot be
        reassigned. We then have the operation name, list of arguments, and the <em>type</em>, which describes
        the types of all
        <Highlight>operands</Highlight>
        on the left of the arrow and the types of all
        <Highlight>results</Highlight>
        on the right. In this case, the type is an <em>unranked</em> tensor of
        <Code>f64</Code> elements, which means we don't know the concrete shape at this point. <em>Ranked</em> tensors
        will have an explicit shape. Finally, as mentioned, all operations have location information. Operations can
        also have
        <Highlight>attributes</Highlight>
        and <Code>regions</Code> associated with it, for example this
        operation which declares a function with a given name and operations:
        <Footnote>
            It is perhaps counterintuitive (it was for me!) that a function is a single operation. In MLIR
            <em>everything</em> is an operation. We will emit all our Toy operations inside a
            <Code>builtin.module</Code> operation representing a module. Functions are operations that have a
            code region attached which represents their body. In fact, since MLIR imposes very few restrictions
            on the ops, you could have an operation that has many code regions and give it whatever semantics
            you want. How to lower those operations to sensible low-level code is a separate concern.
        </Footnote>
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="llvm" LanguageString="MLIR" Code=@(@"
""toy.func""() ({
  ^bb0(%arg0: tensor<*xf64> loc(""toy/valid/ch2-empty.toy"":1:16), %arg1: tensor<*xf64> loc(""toy/valid/ch2-empty.toy"":1:19)):
    ""toy.return""(%2) : (tensor<*xf64>) -> () loc(""toy/valid/ch2-empty.toy"":5:3)
}) {sym_name = ""do_nothing"", type = (tensor<*xf64>, tensor<*xf64>) -> ()} : () -> () loc(""toy/valid/ch2-empty.toy"":1:1)
")>
    </CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        This is the standard assembly format that works with arbitrary operations &ndash; remember that MLIR's set of
        operations is fully extensible &ndash; and we will want to get a less verbose format later on
        (next chapter, more specifically). That being said,
        let's design our dialect by figuring out what operations we will need.
    </Paragraph>
    <OList>
        <ListItem Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ChevronRight">
            <Code Language="llvm">toy.func</Code> &ndash; declares a function with a given identifier and parameter
            list, and holds the block of Toy operations representing the body;
        </ListItem>
        <ListItem Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ChevronRight">
            <Code Language="llvm">toy.generic_call</Code> &ndash; calls a function with a given identifier, passing
            its operands as the arguments;
        </ListItem>
        <ListItem Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ChevronRight">
            <Code Language="llvm">toy.constant</Code> &ndash; representation of a <Code>Literal</Code> which results
            in a single ranked tensor representing the constant;
        </ListItem>
        <ListItem Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ChevronRight">
            <Code Language="llvm">toy.transpose</Code> &ndash; mentioned above, takes a single tensor operand and
            returns it transposed as the result;
        </ListItem>
        <ListItem Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ChevronRight">
            <Code Language="llvm">toy.reshape</Code> &ndash; reinterprets a tensor into a different shape;
            for example, we can reinterpret <Code>[[1, 2], [3, 4]]&lt;2,2&gt;</Code> as
            <Code>[1, 2, 3, 4]&lt;4&gt;</Code>, or <Code>[[1], [2], [3], [4]]&lt;4,1&gt;</Code>, or
            <Code>[[1, 2, 3, 4]]&lt;1,4&gt;</Code>, or <Code>[[[1], [2]], [[3], [4]]]&lt;2,2,1&gt;</Code>, etc. as long
            as the number of elements matches;
        </ListItem>
        <ListItem Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ChevronRight">
            <Code Language="llvm">toy.add</Code> &ndash; adds two operands whose shapes match and returns the result;
        </ListItem>
        <ListItem Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ChevronRight">
            <Code Language="llvm">toy.mul</Code> &ndash; as above, but for element-wise multiplication;
        </ListItem>
        <ListItem Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ChevronRight">
            <Code Language="llvm">toy.print</Code> &ndash; takes a single tensor and prints it; this is the only
            operation that has a side-effect;
        </ListItem>
        <ListItem Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ChevronRight">
            <Code Language="llvm">toy.print</Code> &ndash; takes a single tensor and prints it; this is the only
            operation that has a side-effect;
        </ListItem>
        <ListItem Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ChevronRight">
            <Code Language="llvm">toy.return</Code> &ndash; takes either a single operand and returns it from the
            owning <Code Language="llvm">toy.func</Code>, or no operands (for &ldquo;void&rdquo; functions).
        </ListItem>
    </OList>
    <Paragraph>
        To this end, we create a TableGen file &ndash; <Code>toy.td</Code> &ndash; and define the dialect and the
        base class for our operations:
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" LanguageString="TableGen" FileName="src/dialect/toy.td" Code=@(@"
// Provide a definition of the 'toy' dialect in the ODS framework so that we
// can define our operations.
def Toy_Dialect : Dialect {
  let name = ""toy"";
  let cppNamespace = ""::mlir::toy"";
}

// Base class for toy dialect operations. This operation inherits from the base
// `Op` class in OpBase.td, and provides:
//   * The parent dialect of the operation.
//   * The mnemonic for the operation, or the name without the dialect prefix.
//   * A list of traits for the operation.
class Toy_Op<string mnemonic, list<Trait> traits = []> :
    Op<Toy_Dialect, mnemonic, traits>;
")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        We can now try to define an operation, for example the <Code>toy.transpose</Code>:
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" LanguageString="TableGen" FileName="src/dialect/toy.td" Code=@(@"
def TransposeOp : Toy_Op<""transpose""> {
  let summary = ""transpose operation"";

  let arguments = (ins F64Tensor:$input);
  let results = (outs F64Tensor);

  let assemblyFormat = [{
    `(` $input `:` type($input) `)` attr-dict `to` type(results)
  }];

  // Allow building a TransposeOp from the input operand.
  let builders = [
    OpBuilder<(ins ""Value"":$input)>
  ];

  // Invoke a static verify method to verify this transpose operation.
  let hasVerifier = 1;
}
")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        A couple of things here:
    </Paragraph>
    <OList Dense="true">
        <ListItem Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ChevronRight">
            we declare a single operand and a single result, and the operand gets a name &ndash; <Code>input</Code>;
        </ListItem>
        <ListItem Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ChevronRight">
            the <Code>assemblyFormat</Code> section specifies a pretty-printing format; the code for that will be
            auto-generated to make the assembly output nicer;
        </ListItem>
        <ListItem Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ChevronRight">
            <Code>builders</Code> defines constructors, declaring a specific C++ function on the generated class that
            we are supposed to implement;
        </ListItem>
        <ListItem Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ChevronRight">
            finally, we specify at the end that our class will have a <Code Language="cpp">verify()</Code> function,
            which will be called by the MLIR machinery to make sure the operation is well-formed; in case of transpose
            we want the types of the input and output match like one would expect from a tensor transposition.
        </ListItem>
    </OList>
    <Paragraph>
        TableGen definitions by themselves aren't anything, you need a code generator to actually turn it into useful
        definitions. MLIR comes with a C++ backend, but we've avoided looking at C++ thus far so let's live in that
        world a bit longer. Melior comes with a <Code>dialect</Code> macro that generates Rust code for TableGen,
        so let's see what we get by running it (I cleaned up the autogenerated code to be more readable):
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" Code=@(@"
mod melior_gen {
    melior::dialect! {
        name: ""toy"",
        files: [""src/dialect/toy2.td""]
    }
}
")></CodeBlock>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" FileName="src/dialect/toy.td" Code=@(@"
mod melior_gen {
    pub mod toy {
        use melior::{Context, Error};
        use melior::ir::{Location, Value};
        use melior::ir::operation::{Operation, OperationBuilder, OperationLike, OperationMutLike};
        use std::marker::PhantomData;
        ///A [`transpose`](TransposeOperation) operation. Transpose operation.
        pub struct TransposeOperation<'c> {
            operation: Operation<'c>,
        }
        impl<'c> TransposeOperation<'c> {
            /// Returns a name.
            pub fn name() -> &'static str {
                ""toy.transpose""
            }
            /// Returns a generic operation.
            pub fn as_operation(&self) -> &Operation<'c> {
                &self.operation
            }
            /// Creates a builder.
            pub fn builder(context: &'c Context, location: Location<'c>) -> TransposeOperationBuilder<
                'c,
                ::melior::dialect::ods::__private::Unset,
            > {
                TransposeOperationBuilder::new(context, location)
            }
            pub fn input(&self) -> Result<Value<'c, '_>, Error> {
                self.operation.operand(0)
            }
        }
        ///A builder for a [`transpose`](TransposeOperation) operation.
        pub struct TransposeOperationBuilder<'c, O0> {
            builder: OperationBuilder<'c>,
            context: &'c Context,
            _state: PhantomData<(O0)>,
        }
        impl<'c> TransposeOperationBuilder<'c, ::melior::dialect::ods::__private::Unset> {
            pub fn new(context: &'c Context, location: Location<'c>) -> Self {
                Self {
                    context,
                    builder: OperationBuilder::new(""toy.transpose"", location),
                    _state: PhantomData,
                }
            }
        }
        impl<'c> TransposeOperationBuilder<'c, ::melior::dialect::ods::__private::Unset> {
            pub fn input(self, input: Value<'c, '_>) -> TransposeOperationBuilder<
                'c,
                ::melior::dialect::ods::__private::Set,
            > {
                TransposeOperationBuilder {
                    context: self.context,
                    builder: self.builder.add_operands(&[input]),
                    _state: Default::default(),
                }
            }
        }
        impl<'c> TransposeOperationBuilder<'c, ::melior::dialect::ods::__private::Set> {
            pub fn build(self) -> TransposeOperation<'c> {
                self.builder
                    .build()
                    .expect(""valid operation"")
                    .try_into()
                    .expect(""should be a valid TransposeOperation"")
            }
        }
        #[allow(clippy::too_many_arguments)]
        ///Creates a [`transpose`](TransposeOperation) operation.
        pub fn transpose<'c>(
            context: &'c Context,
            input: Value<'c, '_>,
            location: Location<'c>,
        ) -> TransposeOperation<'c> {
            TransposeOperation::builder(context, location).input(input).build()
        }
        impl<'c> TryFrom<Operation<'c>>
        for TransposeOperation<'c> {
            type Error = Error;
            fn try_from(operation: Operation<'c>) -> Result<Self, Self::Error> {
                Ok(Self { operation })
            }
        }
        impl<'c> From<TransposeOperation<'c>> for Operation<'c> {
            fn from(operation: TransposeOperation<'c>) -> Self {
                operation.operation
            }
        }
    }
}
")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        Sooo&hellip; Ye, I don't like quite a few things here.
    </Paragraph>
    <OList Dense="true">
        <ListItem Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ChevronRight">
            The <Code>hasVerifier</Code> field is ignored &ndash; this will turn out to be the main issue we'll
            have to solve in the next chapter.
        </ListItem>
        <ListItem Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ChevronRight">
            The <Code>input</Code> argument is not verified. In the definition we bound it to be of the
            <Code>F64Tensor</Code> type, but that is not checked anywhere in the builder code.
        </ListItem>
        <ListItem Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ChevronRight">
            You can't see it on this example, but the <Code>builder</Code> field from TableGen is also ignored
            &ndash; Melior always generates a single standard builder function, it just so happens in this case it's
            the same one we wanted.
        </ListItem>
        <ListItem Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ChevronRight">
            There is a <em>big</em> issue that is not immediately visible, but makes those builders actually useless
            &ndash; the operation has no results! The builders should configure the operation to have a single result
            of the <Code>F64Tensor</Code> type, but currently they create an operation with zero results. This makes
            codegen essentially impossible, since MLIR will not bind any values to the transpose.
        </ListItem>
    </OList>
    <Paragraph>
        We're off to a bad start, aren't we? Fortunately, the issues with builder can be fixed by implementing
        our own builder function that sets the results correctly:
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" FileName="src/dialect/toy.rs" LineNumbers="true" LineNumbersStartAt="83" Code=@(@"
/// Create a `toy.transpose` operation.
pub(crate) fn transpose<'c>(
    context: &'c melior::Context,
    input: ir::Value<'c, '_>,
    location: ir::Location<'c>,
) -> ir::Operation<'c> {
    ir::operation::OperationBuilder::new(""toy.transpose"", location)
        .add_operands(&[input])
        .add_results(&[codegen::unranked_tensor_type(context)])
        .build()
        .unwrap()
}")>
    </CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        We'll have to do it for every operation that has any results. We'll deal with verification later.
        Let's setup the other ops, the definitions are taken directly from the MLIR tutorial. Starting with
        the simplest ones:
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" LanguageString="TableGen" FileName="src/dialect/toy.td" Code=@(@"
def AddOp : Toy_Op<""add""> {
  let summary = ""element-wise addition operation"";
  let description = [{
    The ""add"" operation performs element-wise addition between two tensors.
    The shapes of the tensor operands are expected to match.
  }];

  let arguments = (ins F64Tensor:$lhs, F64Tensor:$rhs);
  let results = (outs F64Tensor);

  // Indicate that the operation has a custom parser and printer method.
  let hasCustomAssemblyFormat = 1;

  // Allow building an AddOp with from the two input operands.
  let builders = [
    OpBuilder<(ins ""Value"":$lhs, ""Value"":$rhs)>
  ];
}

def MulOp : Toy_Op<""mul""> {
  let summary = ""element-wise multiplication operation"";
  let description = [{
    The ""mul"" operation performs element-wise multiplication between two
