    <Paragraph>
        The additional values in the definition, like <Code>[Pure]</Code> in the <Code>ConstantOp</Code>, are
        <em>traits</em>. In C++ these are implemented by multiple-inheritance, but conceptually they're close to Rust
        traits. They're used by MLIR for various things &ndash; the <Code>Pure</Code> trait will be useful during
        optimisation, as it tells MLIR these operations can be removed if their values are unused; the
        <Code>Terminator</Code> trait we'll put on <Code>toy.return</Code> to notify MLIR that this operation is a 
        basic block terminator; and <Code>HasParent&lt;"SomeOp"&gt;</Code> enforces that the owner of the region
        in which the operation is located is <Code>SomeOp</Code>.
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph>
        Now there are two operations that contain the <Code>extraClassDeclaration</Code> field.
        This tells MLIR to generate additional functions in the C++ header defining the operation.
        We, of course, have no C++ headers, and Melior does the most sensible thing here and just ignores this field.
        If we want those functions we'll have to implement them ourselves. &ldquo;Custom C++ code in the TableGen&rdquo;
        is going to be a villain of this series, unfortunately. The <Code>FuncOp</Code> additionally contains an
        arbitrary region as its body.
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" LanguageString="TableGen" FileName="src/dialect/toy.td" Code=@(@"
def FuncOp : Toy_Op<""func"", [
    FunctionOpInterface, IsolatedFromAbove
  ]> {
  let summary = ""user defined function operation"";
  let description = [{
    The ""toy.func"" operation represents a user defined function. These are
    callable SSA-region operations that contain toy computations.

    Example:

    ```mlir
    toy.func @main() {
      %0 = toy.constant dense<5.500000e+00> : tensor<f64>
      %1 = toy.reshape(%0 : tensor<f64>) to tensor<2x2xf64>
      toy.print %1 : tensor<2x2xf64>
      toy.return
    }
    ```
  }];

  let arguments = (ins
    SymbolNameAttr:$sym_name,
    TypeAttrOf<FunctionType>:$function_type,
    OptionalAttr<DictArrayAttr>:$arg_attrs,
    OptionalAttr<DictArrayAttr>:$res_attrs
  );
  let regions = (region AnyRegion:$body);

  let builders = [OpBuilder<(ins
    ""StringRef"":$name, ""FunctionType"":$type,
    CArg<""ArrayRef<NamedAttribute>"", ""{}"">:$attrs)
  >];

  let extraClassDeclaration = [{
    //===------------------------------------------------------------------===//
    // FunctionOpInterface Methods
    //===------------------------------------------------------------------===//

    /// Returns the argument types of this function.
    ArrayRef<Type> getArgumentTypes() { return getFunctionType().getInputs(); }

    /// Returns the result types of this function.
    ArrayRef<Type> getResultTypes() { return getFunctionType().getResults(); }

    Region *getCallableRegion() { return &getBody(); }
  }];

  let hasCustomAssemblyFormat = 1;
  let skipDefaultBuilders = 1;
}

def ReturnOp : Toy_Op<""return"", [Pure, HasParent<""FuncOp"">,
                                 Terminator]> {
  let summary = ""return operation"";
  let description = [{
    The ""return"" operation represents a return operation within a function.
    The operation takes an optional tensor operand and produces no results.
    The operand type must match the signature of the function that contains
    the operation. For example:

    ```mlir
      toy.func @foo() -> tensor<2xf64> {
        ...
        toy.return %0 : tensor<2xf64>
      }
    ```
  }];

  // The return operation takes an optional input operand to return. This
  // value must match the return type of the enclosing function.
  let arguments = (ins Variadic<F64Tensor>:$input);

  // The return operation only emits the input in the format if it is present.
  let assemblyFormat = ""($input^ `:` type($input))? attr-dict "";

  // Allow building a ReturnOp with no return operand.
  let builders = [
    OpBuilder<(ins), [{ build($_builder, $_state, {}); }]>
  ];

  // Provide extra utility definitions on the c++ operation class definition.
  let extraClassDeclaration = [{
    bool hasOperand() { return getNumOperands() != 0; }
  }];

  // Invoke a static verify method to verify this return operation.
  let hasVerifier = 1;
}")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph GutterBottom="false">
        Now there's one more issue with the <Code>dialect</Code> macro and the Melior API, namely that there is no
        hook to register our dialect with the MLIR Context used to generate code. This is an issue for the next chapter,
        and we actually don't need it to generate some code.
    </Paragraph>
    <Header2>Codegen!</Header2>
    <Paragraph>
        We can now create the codegen module that will take an AST of Toy and convert it into MLIR bytecode using
        the Toy dialect. MLIR requires a Context to be constructed first. Every other MLIR object is tied to the
        context, which means its lifetime is pervasive across all values, operations, blocks, etc. We are going
        to consistently use <Code>'c</Code> for the captured Context lifetime.
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph>
        First we wrap the Context into a custom <Code>MlirGenerator</Code> struct which will initialise all MLIR things
        in its constructor. To actually perform the codegen we will create a transient <Code>Codegen&lt;'c&gt;</Code>
        struct that holds a Context reference and the MLIR module being built.
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="Rust" FileName="src/codegen.rs" Code=@(@"
// Rename our location type as AstLocation to not confuse it with the IR melior::ir::Location.
use crate::Location as AstLocation;

/// MLIR context capable of generating Toy bytecode.
pub struct MlirGenerator {
    context: melior::Context,
}

/// Active codegen instance that builds the inner `module` step-by-step.
struct Codegen<'c> {
    context: &'c melior::Context,
    module: ir::Module<'c>,
}
impl MlirGenerator {
    /// Create a new MLIR context and initialize Toy code generation.
    #[must_use]
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        use melior::{
            Context,
            dialect::DialectRegistry,
            utility::register_all_dialects,
        };
        let context = Context::new();
        // Register all standard dialects.
        let registry = DialectRegistry::new();
        register_all_dialects(&registry);

        // Allow using the Toy dialect even though we don't register it with the context.
        context.set_allow_unregistered_dialects(true);
        context.append_dialect_registry(&registry);
        context.load_all_available_dialects();

        Self { context }
    }

    /// Generate Toy MLIR code for the given module.
    pub fn generate_mlir(&self, module_ast: &ModuleAst) -> Result<ir::Module<'_>, CodegenError> {
        let codegen = Codegen::new(&self.context, module_ast.loc());
        codegen.generate_module(module_ast)
    }
}

impl<'c> Codegen<'c> {
    /// Initialize a new module to codegen for.
    pub(crate) fn new(context: &'c melior::Context, location: &AstLocation) -> Self {
        let module = ir::Module::new(location.mlir_loc(context));
        Self { context, module }
    }

    /// Entrypoint for codegen - take [`ModuleAst`], generate MLIR, return the module and consume
    /// this [`Codegen`] instance.
    pub(crate) fn generate_module(mut self, module_ast: &ModuleAst) -> Result<ir::Module<'c>, CodegenError> {
        todo!()
    }
}
")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        The <Code>mlir_loc</Code> is a simple utility to convert our AST locations to MLIR locations that require a
        context reference.
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" FileName="src/lib.rs" Code=@(@"
impl Location {
    /// Turn this location into an MLIR location.
    #[must_use]
    pub fn mlir_loc<'c>(&self, context: &'c melior::Context) -> melior::ir::Location<'c> {
        melior::ir::Location::new(context, self.file.as_ref(), self.line, self.col)
    }
}")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        Because our dialect captures Toy operations on a rather high level, the translation from AST to
        Toy IR is actually pretty straightforward. We will generate code for each function separately.
        We have no control flow in our language other than function calls, so all functions will consist of a single
        block. We need a symbol table (known as an <em>environment</em>) to be able to resolve previously
        declared variables, including function parameters.
        A simple hashmap would do, but I always prefer to use an <em>immutable</em> (a.k.a. <em>persistent</em>)<Footnote>
            A persistent data structure supports updates by creating a new copy of the structure whenever it is updated,
            and keeping the original intact. This sounds inefficient, but we can implement it using sharing, e.g. most
            nodes of a binary-tree-based map remain the same after an insertion and can be shared between older copies.
            Effectively implemented persistent hashmap will have $\mathcal{O}(\log n)$ access time.
        </Footnote>
        to allow scoping. In our case there are only two scopes &ndash; global module scope and function scope,
        and the former is empty.<Footnote>Technically, the global scope contains symbols for all the functions.
            However, we are not going to be binding function names in this first codegen pass. The
            <Code>toy.generic_call</Code> can be generated without knowing anything about the callee,
            so we don't care if they even exist. We <em>could</em> check if the name is declared, but that
            responsibility is better left to later stages of codegen where the call is actually bound and we would have
            to ensure it exists for the second time anyway.
        </Footnote>
        When entering a function we create a new scope and declare all
        parameters. When we're finished, we simply drop the scope, and everything works out automagically due to the
        immutable data structure underneath. We use the
        <LinkA Href="https://github.com/bodil/im-rs"><Code>im</Code></LinkA>
        crate<Footnote IsRef="true"><LinkA Href="https://github.com/bodil/im-rs">GitHub: bodil/im-rs</LinkA></Footnote>
        for this<Footnote>The <Code>im::HashMap</Code> is actually <em>not</em> immutable in the Rust sense,
            as it has methods that take a mutable reference and do modify the underlying structure.
            Under the hood it keeps the structure persistent, though, and modifications in one copy are not visible
            to any other copies. This actually makes it more ergonomic; in our case we can keep a single environment
            for the current scope and add newly declared variables as we go using a regular mutable interface.
            Since our language doesn't have nested scopes we could just use a regular
            <Code Language="rust">std::collections::HashMap</Code>
            here, but this way adding more complex scoping to Toy would be very straightforward. The official MLIR
            Toy tutorial also uses a stack of scopes, so we're keeping with the source material.
        </Footnote>
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="Rust" FileName="src/codegen/env.rs" LineNumbers="true" Code=@(@"
//! Environment for declaring variables in a scope during codegen.
use crate::Location as AstLocation;
use crate::codegen::error::CodegenError;
use im::hashmap::Entry;
use melior::ir;
use std::sync::Arc;

/// Variable environment - persistent hashmap mapping identifiers to their MLIR value and location.
#[derive(Clone)]
pub(super) struct Env<'c, 'a>(im::HashMap<Arc<String>, (ir::Value<'c, 'a>, AstLocation)>);

impl<'a, 'c> Env<'c, 'a> {
    /// Create an empty environment.
    #[must_use]
    pub(super) fn new() -> Self {
        Self(im::HashMap::new())
    }

    /// Declare a variable within the environment.
    pub(super) fn declare(
        &mut self,
        var: Arc<String>,
        value: ir::Value<'c, 'a>,
        loc: &AstLocation,
    ) -> Result<(), CodegenError> {
        match self.0.entry(var) {
            Entry::Occupied(entry) => Err(CodegenError::declaration_shadowing(
                entry.key().as_ref(),
                &entry.get().1,
                loc,
            )),
            Entry::Vacant(slot) => {
                slot.insert((value, loc.clone()));
                Ok(())
            }
        }
    }

    /// Lookup a variable that was previously declared.
    #[must_use]
    pub(super) fn lookup(&self, var: &String) -> Option<&(ir::Value<'c, 'a>, AstLocation)> {
        self.0.get(var)
    }
}
")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        To generate the module we just generate <Code>toy.func</Code> ops one by one, and then run
        <Highlight>MLIR passes</Highlight>
        on the generated code. Passes are what optimises and gradually changes the
        IR into the end result. For now, the only pass is going to be the
        <Highlight>verifier</Highlight>, which
        will verify (duh) that all operations we generate conform to restrictions specified in the TableGen
        (e.g. <Code>toy.return</Code> must have a <Code>toy.func</Code> parent), as well as running the
        <Code>verify</Code> functions that are currently defined in <Code>toy.cpp</Code>.
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" FileName="src/codegen.rs" Code=@(@"
impl<'c> Codegen<'c> {
    /* ... ✂️ snip ... */
    /// Entrypoint for codegen - take [`ModuleAst`], generate MLIR, return the module and consume
    /// this [`Codegen`] instance.
    pub(crate) fn generate_module(mut self, module_ast: &ModuleAst) -> Result<ir::Module<'c>, CodegenError> {
        for f_ast in module_ast.functions() {
            self.generate_function(f_ast)?;
        }

        let pass_manager = melior::pass::PassManager::new(self.context);
        pass_manager.enable_verifier(true);

        pass_manager.run(&mut self.module)?;

        if !self.module.as_operation().verify() {
            return Err(CodegenError::verify_module_failed(module_ast.loc()));
        }

        Ok(self.module)
    }
}")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        To generate a function we need to look at the prototype and compute the initial function type. The actual
        function type depends on the <Code>toy.return</Code> function within the body &ndash; if it has an operand,
        then the operand's type is the return type; otherwise, it's void. Therefore, we need to generate the op first,
        remember the argument types, and then modify the type after we go through the body and find the return
        statement.
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" FileName="src/codegen.rs" Code=@(@"
/// Intermediate function prototype.
///
/// This is required because we need to generate the toy.func operation first based on parameter
/// types, then the body, and then come back and figure out the result type of the func based
/// on `toy.return` found in the body.
struct FuncProto<'c> {{
    context: &'c melior::Context,
    func_op: toy::FuncOperation<'c>,
    arg_types: Vec<ir::Type<'c>>,
}}

impl<'c> Codegen<'c> {
    /* ... ✂️ snip ... */

    /// Generate a toy.func instance with an empty body and a single entry block.
    /// The type of the function is void initially, but can be later changed via the returned
    /// [`FuncProto`].
    fn generate_prototype(&mut self, proto_ast: &PrototypeAst) -> Result<FuncProto<'c>, CodegenError> {
        use ir::{
            attribute::{StringAttribute, TypeAttribute},
            r#type::FunctionType,
        };

        let loc = proto_ast.loc().mlir_loc(self.context);
        let args = proto_ast
            .args()
            .iter()
            .map(|a| (self.mlir_type_from(&vec![]), a.loc().mlir_loc(self.context)))
            .collect::<Vec<_>>();
        let arg_types = args.iter().map(|(a, _)| *a).collect::<Vec<_>>();
        let f_type = FunctionType::new(self.context, &arg_types, &[]);
        let entry_block = ir::Block::new(&args);
        let region = ir::Region::new();
        region.append_block(entry_block);

        let f_op = toy::func(
            self.context,
            region,
            StringAttribute::new(self.context, proto_ast.name()),
            TypeAttribute::new(f_type.into()),
            loc,
        );

        Ok(FuncProto::new(self.context, f_op, arg_types))
    }

    /// Get the type corresponding to the given shape.
    /// This is UnrankedTensorType for empty shapes, RankedTensorType of given dimensions
    /// for a non-empty shape.
    fn mlir_type_from(&self, shape: &Shape) -> ir::Type<'c> {
        if shape.is_empty() {
            unranked_tensor_type(self.context)
        } else {
            ir::r#type::RankedTensorType::new(shape, f64_type(self.context), None).into()
        }
    }
}
")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        For the actual function we need to prepare the environment to contain all parameters, then generate the body
        and investigate the return statement. We can find the return by checking the terminator of its only block.
        Recall that each block must have exactly one terminator, so if there was no explicit return statement in the
        Toy source we need to generate a void return op manually.
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" FileName="src/codegen.rs" Code=@(@"
impl<'c> Codegen<'c> {
    /* ... ✂️ snip ... */

    /// Generate all code for a given function.
    fn generate_function(&mut self, f_ast: &FunctionAst) -> Result<(), CodegenError> {
        let mut func_proto = self.generate_prototype(f_ast.proto())?;
        let block = func_proto.func_op.body()?.first_block().expect(""function to have body"");
        let args = f_ast.proto().args();
        {
            let mut env = Env::new();
            for (arg, value) in args.iter().zip(block.arguments()) {
                env.declare(arg.name().clone(), value.into(), arg.loc())?;
            }

            self.generate_function_body(f_ast.body(), &env, block)?;
            drop(env); // Forget all values in the block so that we can modify it now.

            match block.terminator() {
                Some(t) if t.operand_count() > 0 => {
                    func_proto.set_return_type(unranked_tensor_type(self.context));
                }
                Some(_) => (),
                None => {
                    let op = toy::r#return(self.context, &[], f_ast.proto().loc().mlir_loc(self.context));
                    block.append_operation(op.into());
                }
            }
        }

        self.module.body().append_operation(func_proto.func_op.into());

        Ok(())
    }

    /// Generate code for the `exprs` of a function. The `env` should have all function formal
    /// parameters declared, and `block` should be the functions entry point.
    fn generate_function_body<'a>(
        &mut self,
        exprs: &[TopLevelExprAst],
        env: &Env<'c, 'a>,
        block: ir::BlockRef<'c, 'a>,
    ) -> Result<(), CodegenError> {
        let mut body_scope = env.clone();
        for expr in exprs {
            match expr {
                TopLevelExprAst::VarDecl(d) => self.generate_var_decl(d, &mut body_scope, block)?,
                TopLevelExprAst::Return(d) => self.generate_return(d, &body_scope, block)?,
                TopLevelExprAst::Print(d) => self.generate_print(d, &body_scope, block)?,
                TopLevelExprAst::Expr(expr) => {
                    self.generate_generic_expr(expr, &body_scope, block)?;
                }
            }
        }

        Ok(())
    }
}
")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        For a variable declaration we generate the value from the initialisation expression and then register it in the
        environment. Additionally, if an explicit type was given we emit a reshape to match that type. Return and print
        are straightforward, while for expressions we branch based on the exact expr type.
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" FileName="src/codegen.rs" Code=@(@"
impl<'c> Codegen<'c> {
    /* ... ✂️ snip ... */

    /// Generate a variable declaration and put it into `env`.
    fn generate_var_decl<'a>(
        &mut self,
        data: &VarDeclData,
        env: &mut Env<'c, 'a>,
        block: ir::BlockRef<'c, 'a>,
    ) -> Result<(), CodegenError> {
        let mut val = self.generate_generic_expr(data.init_val(), env, block)?;

        // If the variable has an explicit type, emit a `toy.reshape` to coerce to that type.
        // Unnecessary reshapes will be removed by optimization passes later.
        if let Some(ty) = data.ty() {
            let op = toy::reshape(
                self.context,
                val,
                self.mlir_type_from(ty.shape()),
                data.loc().mlir_loc(self.context),
            );
            val = op.result(0)?.into();
            block.append_operation(op);
        }

        env.declare(data.name(), val, data.loc())?;

        Ok(())
    }

    /// Generate a return operation.
    fn generate_return<'a>(
        &mut self,
        data: &ReturnData,
        env: &Env<'c, 'a>,
        block: ir::BlockRef<'c, 'a>,
    ) -> Result<(), CodegenError> {
        let op = if let Some(ret_expr) = data.expr() {
            // return <expr>;
            let val = self.generate_generic_expr(ret_expr, env, block)?;
            toy::r#return(self.context, &[val], data.loc().mlir_loc(self.context))
        } else {
            // return;
            toy::r#return(self.context, &[], data.loc().mlir_loc(self.context))
        };
        block.append_operation(op.into());

        Ok(())
    }

    /// Generate a call to the builtin `print`.
    fn generate_print<'a>(
        &mut self,
        data: &PrintData,
        env: &Env<'c, 'a>,
        block: ir::BlockRef<'c, 'a>,
    ) -> Result<(), CodegenError> {
        // `print` is handled via a special operation.
        let arg = self.generate_generic_expr(data.arg(), env, block)?;
        let op = toy::print(self.context, arg, data.loc().mlir_loc(self.context));

        block.append_operation(op.into());

        Ok(())
    }

    /// Generate a generic expression, i.e. something that evaluates to a value and is not
    /// a declaration, return, or print.
    fn generate_generic_expr<'a>(
        &mut self,
        expr: &ExprAst,
        env: &Env<'c, 'a>,
        block: ir::BlockRef<'c, 'a>,
    ) -> Result<ir::Value<'c, 'a>, CodegenError> {
        match expr {
            ExprAst::Num(data) => self.generate_num_expr(data, block),
            ExprAst::Literal(data) => self.generate_literal_expr(data, block),
            ExprAst::Var(data) => Self::generate_var_expr(data, env),
            ExprAst::BinOp(data) => self.generate_binop(data, env, block),
            ExprAst::Call(data) => self.generate_call(data, env, block),
        }
    }
}
")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        Numerical expressions are straightforward, there's just a lot of busywork required to construct the appropriate
        attribute for MLIR. Binary operations recursively create the lhs and rhs values and then combine them.
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" FileName="src/codegen.rs" Code=@(@"
impl<'c> Codegen<'c> {
    /* ... ✂️ snip ... */

    /// Generate a constant numerical expression.
    fn generate_num_expr<'a>(
        &mut self,
        data: &NumData,
        block: ir::BlockRef<'c, 'a>,
    ) -> Result<ir::Value<'c, 'a>, CodegenError> {
        use melior::ir::attribute::FloatAttribute;
        use melior::ir::r#type::RankedTensorType;

        // Single-number constants are still represented as RankedTensorType values, just with
        // no dimensions.
        let f64_ty = f64_type(self.context);
        let ty = RankedTensorType::new(&[], f64_ty, None);
        let f64_attr = FloatAttribute::new(self.context, f64_ty, data.value());
        let op = toy::constant(self.context, ty, &[f64_attr.into()], data.loc().mlir_loc(self.context));
        let val = block.append_operation(op).result(0)?;
        Ok(val.into())
    }    

    /// Generate code for a binary operation.
    fn generate_binop<'a>(
        &mut self,
        data: &BinOpData,
        env: &Env<'c, 'a>,
        block: ir::BlockRef<'c, 'a>,
    ) -> Result<ir::Value<'c, 'a>, CodegenError> {
        // First generate the left- and right-hand-side values.
        let lhs = self.generate_generic_expr(data.lhs(), env, block)?;
        let rhs = self.generate_generic_expr(data.rhs(), env, block)?;

        // Emit the correct operation depending on the operator.
        match data.op() {
            Op::Add => {
                let op = toy::add(self.context, lhs, rhs, data.loc().mlir_loc(self.context));
                let val = op.result(0)?;
                block.append_operation(op);
                Ok(val.into())
            }
            Op::Multiply => {
                let op = toy::mul(self.context, lhs, rhs, data.loc().mlir_loc(self.context));
                let val = op.result(0)?;
                block.append_operation(op);
                Ok(val.into())
            }
        }
    }
}
")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        For calls we need to generate the values for all arguments. We also special-case the <Code>transpose</Code>
        function.<Footnote>In my opinion
            it'd be better to make <Code>transpose</Code> a keyword at the syntax level, just like <Code>print</Code>
            is. Right now there's nothing stopping a user from creating their own <Code>transpose</Code> function
            or defining a variable called <Code>transpose</Code>, which would lead to confusion. I'm sticking
            to how the original Toy tutorial does it here, though.</Footnote>
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" FileName="src/codegen.rs" Code=@(@"
impl<'c> Codegen<'c> {
    /* ... ✂️ snip ... */

    /// Generate a function call.
    fn generate_call<'a>(
        &mut self,
        data: &CallData,
        env: &Env<'c, 'a>,
        block: ir::BlockRef<'c, 'a>,
    ) -> Result<ir::Value<'c, 'a>, CodegenError> {
        // First we generate values for all arguments.
        let mut operands = Vec::with_capacity(data.args().len());
        for arg in data.args() {
            let val = self.generate_generic_expr(arg, env, block)?;
            operands.push(val);
        }

        // `transpose` has special handling and maps to toy.transpose
        if data.callee().as_ref() == ""transpose"" {
            // `transpose` is unary.
            if operands.len() > 1 {
                Err(CodegenError::invalid_transpose_arguments(operands.len(), data.loc()))
            } else {
                let op = toy::transpose(self.context, operands[0], data.loc().mlir_loc(self.context));
                let val = op.result(0)?;
                block.append_operation(op);
                Ok(val.into())
            }
        } else {
            // generate a generic call
            let op = toy::generic_call(
                self.context,
                &operands,
                data.callee().as_ref(),
                data.loc().mlir_loc(self.context),
            );
            let val = op.result(0)?;
            block.append_operation(op);
            Ok(val.into())
        }
    }
}
")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        Literal expressions are more tedious than anything. We need to create a separate <Code>FloatAttribute</Code>
        for each individual numerical value, which we need to gather recursively through the literal AST. We also need
        the type attribute for the overall literal value, and finally emit the constant op.
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" FileName="src/codegen.rs" Code=@(@"
impl<'c> Codegen<'c> {
